 # HumanNeRF
 
-Also called: Generalizable Dynamic Neural Radiance Field for Humans
+Also called: HumanNeRF
 
 ## Overview
-HumanNeRF is a neural representation for high-fidelity free-view synthesis of dynamic humans that generalizes to unseen subjects and camera settings using sparse RGB inputs, avoiding per-scene training through aggregated pixel-alignment features and a pose-embedded non-rigid deformation field [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like [Unified Volumetric Avatar (UVA)](13784-unified-volumetric-avatar-uva.md) and [HumanNeRF-SE](15640-humannerf-se.md) by explicitly combining image-based rendering with NeRF-based volume rendering via a neural appearance blending scheme to preserve high-frequency texture details that are often lost in purely implicit representations [S1].
+HumanNeRF is a generalizable neural radiance field method for high-fidelity free-view synthesis of dynamic humans from sparse multi-view inputs, designed to generalize to unseen subjects and camera settings without per-scene training [S1]. It distinguishes itself from prior methods by combining a generalizable neural radiance field with an aggregated pixel-alignment feature extraction and a novel neural appearance blending scheme, addressing the blur artifacts and geometric discontinuities common in implicit texture representations [S1]. Unlike methods requiring dense inputs or costly per-frame optimization, HumanNeRF leverages a pre-trained network on large human datasets to enable efficient generalization, with an optional fast per-scene fine-tuning stage to refine details [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** $K$ synchronized video frames of a performer captured at different viewpoints (preferably 360°), denoted as $I = \{I_{k,t}\}$, where $k \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ is the view index and $t$ is the frame index. Each input image $I_k \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 4}$ includes the RGB color and an alpha mask as the last channel [S1].
-*   **Output:** Synthesized RGB images and depth maps for novel viewpoints and arbitrary poses [S1].
+*   **Input:** $K$ synchronized videos of a performer captured at different viewpoints (preferably 360°) with $T$ frames, denoted as $I = \{I_{k,t}\}$, where each image $I_k \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 4}$ includes the mask as the last channel [S1].
+*   **Output:** Synthesized RGB images and depth/volume density for novel viewpoints and arbitrary poses [S1].
 
